  params: [
    { name: 'vmax', label: 'max rate Vmax', min: 20, max: 100, step: 5, value: 60 },
    { name: 'km', label: 'Km (substrate at half-max)', min: 20, max: 200, step: 10, value: 80 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 400, label: 'substrate concentration' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 100, label: 'rate' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const tempCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '30*x*exp(-x/12)/12', label: 'enzyme activity vs temperature', color: '#2e7d32', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: '28*x*exp(-x/40)/40', label: 'denatured enzyme (higher optimum)', color: '#b42318', width: 2 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 80, label: 'temperature (°C)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 30, label: 'rate' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 320,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/enzymes-and-metabolism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You already know that cells are bundles of chemistry — respiration, protein building, DNA
    repair — all running at the mild temperature of a warm bath. Under those conditions almost none
    of those reactions should happen: the bonds involved are simply too stable for random molecular
    collisions to break them at any useful speed. Life's answer is the <strong>enzyme</strong>: a
    protein folded into a shape that grips a specific molecule and physically strains it toward
    reaction. An enzyme does not supply energy and does not change what a reaction produces — it
    lowers the <em>activation energy</em>, the energy hill that reactants must climb. Typical
    enzyme-catalysed reactions run 10⁶ to 10¹² times faster than the uncatalysed ones; carbonic
    anhydrase hydrates around a million CO₂ molecules per second per enzyme, which is why your
    blood can haul carbon dioxide at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Metabolism</strong> is the sum of all these catalysed reactions: a network of thousands
    of steps, each owned by one enzyme, all regulated so the cell builds what it needs, when it
    needs it, without cooking itself. Master one enzyme and you understand the whole network's
    logic.
  </p>

  <h2>Enzyme action: the active site and the lock-and-key model</h2>
  <p>
    Every enzyme has an <strong>active site</strong> — a small pocket, typically involving just 3–12
    amino acid side chains, formed by the protein's precise 3-D folding. Only a substrate whose
    shape and chemical groups complement the pocket fits, which is why enzymes are
    <strong>specific</strong>: amylase attacks starch but ignores cellulose, even though both are
    glucose polymers, because the geometry of the bonds differs. The classic description is
    <strong>lock and key</strong>, but the modern refinement is <em>induced fit</em>: the substrate
    binds loosely, then the enzyme moulds around it, clamping it into a strained, reactive
    conformation. The catalysis is physical as much as chemical — acid side chains donate protons,
    charged groups hold the substrate's own charges apart, and the straining itself stretches bonds
    toward their breaking point.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example puts numbers on specificity. Sucrase hydrolyses sucrose at thousands of
    reactions per second per enzyme. Without the enzyme, hydrolysing sucrose in a neutral aqueous
    solution at 25 °C takes a half-time measured in <em>centuries</em>. The enzyme provides no new
    chemistry — the same hydrolysis occurs — it just makes the transition state accessible. That is
    also why enzymes emerge unchanged at the end of each reaction: they are equipment, not
    ingredients, and a single enzyme molecule may catalyse millions of reactions before it is
    degraded and replaced.
  </p>

  <h2>Substrate concentration: the Michaelis–Menten curve</h2>
  <p>
    Watch what happens as you feed an enzyme more and more substrate. At low concentrations, rate
    rises almost in proportion — most active sites are empty, so every added substrate molecule
    finds an enzyme waiting. As substrate increases, more active sites become occupied and the rate
    levels off at <strong>Vmax</strong>: every enzyme is now working flat out, and adding more
    substrate can't help. The concentration at which rate reaches exactly half of Vmax is called
    <strong>Km</strong>, and it's a genuine physical constant of that enzyme–substrate pair: a low
    Km means a tight grip (half-saturation at tiny substrate levels), a high Km means a loose one.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Michaelis–Menten curve — fast at first, then saturated; drag Vmax and Km like a mutation would" widget="plotter" config={enzymeCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the graph like a physiologist. With Vmax = 60 and Km = 80 (arbitrary units), the rate at a
    substrate concentration of 80 is 30 — half of Vmax, exactly as the definition demands. A
    mutation that doubles Km widens the curve's knee: the enzyme now needs four times the substrate
    to reach the same output. This is not a textbook abstraction — many inherited metabolic
    diseases are enzymes with increased Km, and drug design is largely the craft of deliberately
    wrecking a pathogen's Km values.
  </p>

  <h2>Temperature and pH: why enzymes are fragile</h2>
  <p>
    Two environmental dials control every enzyme, and both produce characteristic curves.
    <strong>Temperature</strong> helps and hurts at once: warming gives molecules more kinetic
    energy so collisions are more frequent and energetic (roughly doubling rates per 10 °C rise),
    but beyond a limit the heat shakes the protein's weak bonds — hydrogen bonds, ionic
    interactions — and the folded shape <strong>denatures</strong>. The active site loses its
    geometry and the enzyme stops for good. Human enzymes typically peak near 37–40 °C; the
    thermophile bacterium <em>Thermus aquaticus</em> lives at 70 °C and its DNA polymerase survives
    the 95 °C of a PCR machine, which is why the whole biotech industry runs on Taq. That fever is
    dangerous above about 41 °C is a direct consequence: your own enzymes begin to denature.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="activity against temperature — a kinetic climb, then a denaturation cliff; the red curve is a heat-adapted variant" widget="plotter" config={tempCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>pH</strong> matters because the charged groups that hold the active site's shape and do
    the chemistry depend on protonation. Pepsin in the stomach has an optimum near pH 2, trypsin in
    the small intestine near pH 8, and cytoplasmic enzymes cluster around pH 7. Move an enzyme one
    or two pH units from its optimum and activity collapses — not because the protein falls apart
    permanently (that's denaturation), but because its catalytic side chains have gained or lost
    protons and can no longer form the right interactions. Blood is buffered within 0.05 pH units
    of 7.4 precisely because haemoglobin and every enzyme of respiration are pH-tuned to that
    value.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "enzymes are killed" by heat. Enzymes are proteins, not
    organisms — they don't die, they <strong>denature</strong>, a physical change of shape. That's
    why cooling rather than reheating preserves them: a denatured enzyme almost never refolds, but
    a cold one simply slows down and recovers full activity when warmed.</p>

  <h2>Measuring rates, and the helpers enzymes need</h2>
  <p>
    Enzyme experiments are staple practical work, and the technique is simple in principle: pick
    something you can watch, hold everything else constant, and time it. Amylase digesting starch
    can be tracked by sampling drops into iodine — the blue-black colour fades as starch
    disappears. Catalase decomposing hydrogen peroxide releases oxygen gas you can count in a
    foam column or gas syringe. The discipline is in the controls: run the experiment without
    enzyme (or with boiled enzyme) to prove the reaction you see is really catalysis, and repeat at
    each temperature or pH to plot the curves above properly. One subtlety worth learning early:
    measure the <em>initial</em> rate, in the first few seconds or minutes. As substrate is used up
    the rate naturally falls, so late measurements mix up enzyme performance with substrate
    shortage — exactly the confusion the Michaelis–Menten framework is designed to avoid.
  </p>
  <p>
    Many enzymes also refuse to work alone. <strong>Cofactors</strong> are non-protein partners
    they require: metal ions such as zinc in carbonic anhydrase, iron in catalase, magnesium
    stabilising ATP-binding sites. <strong>Coenzymes</strong> are organic cofactors that shuttle
    small molecules between enzymes — NAD carries hydrogen in respiration, coenzyme A carries
    acetyl groups into the Krebs cycle. Vitamins are coenzyme precursors, which is why a dietary
    deficiency can stall a specific metabolic step: no vitamin B₁, no working decarboxylase
    enzymes, and the nervous system fails. Prosthetic groups (like the iron-containing haem in
    catalase) are coenzymes bound permanently to their enzyme. The pattern to notice: the protein
    provides shape and specificity, the cofactor provides chemistry the amino acids alone cannot
    perform.
  </p>

  <h2>How metabolism is organised</h2>
  <p>
    Individual reactions are chained into <strong>pathways</strong> — glycolysis, the Krebs cycle,
    the urea cycle — where the product of one enzyme becomes the substrate of the next. This
    assembly-line structure creates regulation points: the first enzyme of a pathway is usually the
    one that's controlled, because shutting it down stops the whole line cheaply. The classic
    mechanism is <strong>feedback (end-product) inhibition</strong>: when isoleucine builds up, some
    of it binds to an allosteric site on threonine deaminase, the first enzyme of its synthesis
    pathway, forcing the enzyme into an inactive shape. Production halts within seconds; when
    isoleucine is consumed, inhibition lifts. Allosteric regulation — control from a site other
    than the active one — is how cells switch whole pathways on and off without touching their
    genes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Slower control operates on <strong>enzyme quantity</strong>: genes encoding a given enzyme are
    transcribed more or less avidly depending on need, changing the enzyme supply over minutes to
    hours. And because many useful reactions are energetically unfavourable, cells
    <strong>couple</strong> them to favourable ones through ATP — the universal molecular currency.
    Building one peptide bond costs roughly one ATP; a growing bacterial cell spends on the order of
    a billion ATP molecules per second, all regenerated by respiration. Life never gets free
    energy — it channels it (the photosynthesis lesson shows where the channel starts).
  </p>
  <p>
    Medicine is built on this biochemistry. <strong>Statins</strong> competitively block HMG-CoA
    reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis. <strong>Aspirin</strong>
    permanently disables COX enzymes, cutting the production of inflammatory prostaglandins.
    <strong>Penicillin</strong> jamms the transpeptidase that cross-links bacterial cell walls.
    <strong>Cyanide</strong> poisons cytochrome c oxidase, and the victim dies of internal
    suffocation — cells that cannot use oxygen. Pharmacology is largely enzyme interference with a
    spec sheet.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "enzymes work everywhere in the cell equally." They don't.
    Enzymes are not dissolved uniformly — they are anchored to membranes, packed into organelles,
    and assembled into multi-enzyme complexes, so substrates are handed from one enzyme to the
    next like parts on a conveyor. That spatial organisation is a third layer of control alongside
    feedback inhibition and gene expression: a pathway can be physically isolated in the
    mitochondrion (the Krebs cycle) or the cytoplasm (glycolysis) so its intermediates never mix.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The Michaelis–Menten equation, rate = Vmax·[S] / (Km + [S]), falls out of a simple
    steady-state assumption: after a brief initial period, the enzyme–substrate complex forms and
    breaks down at equal rates, so its concentration is roughly constant. Real enzymes complicate
    this tidy picture. <strong>Allosteric enzymes</strong> show sigmoid (S-shaped) rate curves
    rather than hyperbolas, because substrate binding at one subunit changes the affinity of the
    others — cooperative binding, the same logic that makes haemoglobin's oxygen curve sigmoidal.
    <strong>Competitive inhibitors</strong> compete for the active site and raise apparent Km
    without touching Vmax — flooding the system with substrate overcomes them;
    <strong>non-competitive inhibitors</strong> bind elsewhere and lower Vmax instead. Distinguishing
    the two experimentally (rate curves at several inhibitor doses) is a standard drug-discovery
    assay.
  </p>
  <p>
    Enzyme kinetics also sets real biological limits. An enzyme's <em>turnover number</em> (kcat)
    tops out near 10⁷ per second because diffusion itself limits how fast substrate can arrive —
    catalase, at about 4 × 10⁷ per second, is effectively diffusion-limited, meaning it is as fast
    as chemistry permits. Evolution has optimised some enzymes to the physical ceiling. And
    enzymes are not always proteins: ribozymes — catalytic RNA — exist (peptidyl transferase in the
    ribosome is one), and their discovery reshaped theories about the origin of life, since RNA can
    carry information and catalyse at once. Even industrial chemistry has co-opted them: biological
    washing powders use proteases and lipases that work in cool water, and glucose-fructose syrup
    — sweetening a large share of the world's soft drinks — is made by immobilised isomerase
    enzymes in factory-sized reactors.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Michaelis &amp; Menten (1913); Berg, Tymoczko &amp; Stryer, Biochemistry; OpenStax, Biology 2e (Metabolism); Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus, unit 5.</p>
</TopicPage>
